# MalloySim scenario: rotating equilateral three-body (Lagrange) configuration.
# Three equal masses on a triangle, with velocities for near-rigid rotation.

g 1.0
softening 0.001
dt 0.001
steps 10000
output_every 1000

body 1.0   1.0   0.0         0.0         0.75984
body 1.0  -0.5   0.8660254  -0.6580407399359999  -0.37992
body 1.0  -0.5  -0.8660254   0.6580407399359999  -0.37992

# Velocities are carried at full precision (0.8660254 * omega), not rounded, so
# this file reproduces the built-in three-body demo exactly.
#
# Expected final report (Debug and Release, MSVC):
#   step  10000   sep  1.73143038   E_total  -8.66015093e-01   L_total   2.27951999e+00

# Machine-checkable assertions, evaluated against a real run by the scenario
# tests (issue #14). `step 0` is the state before any step, matching the column
# the app prints, and the tolerance is absolute.
#
# The final energy quoted above is checked, not just quoted (issue #67), and
# this template is the one that really guards the force law. Angular momentum
# is exact under this integrator for any central pairwise force, so weakening
# the force law to 1/r (the acceleration's inv_r^3 written as inv_r^2) passed
# every check in both nbody templates. Here that mutation gives
# -7.30738819e-01, moving the step-10000 energy by 0.135, about 1.4e8
# tolerances, because the three separations are 1.73 rather than near 1 where
# the two laws nearly agree.
# Changing dt by a factor of 4, or by one per cent either way, also fails it.
#
# Tolerance: the figure is identical on MSVC Release and clang 21 Release to all
# nine printed digits, so the printing binds, and 1e-9 is twice half an ulp of
# the last digit of a value near -0.87.
# check step 0    energy     -8.66015288e-01   tol 1e-8
# check step 0    angular     2.27951999e+00   tol 1e-8
# check step 10000 angular    2.27951999e+00   tol 1e-8
# check step 10000 energy    -8.66015093e-01   tol 1e-9
